27:- `interactions(tree)` — pure `ThreadTree -> Interaction[]`, DFS, parents before children.
47:**Slice the tree into interactions.** `interactions(tree)` walks depth-first from the root, parents before children, and emits one `Interaction` per user turn. Two kinds of user turn exist and both must be captured:
52:**Context is the whole thread prefix.** The cheap thing — sending only the assistant blocks either side of the turn — is an optimization, and optimizing before we know whether the model can produce good notes at all is backwards. So each interaction is reflected on with everything that led to it: the breadcrumb of ancestor threads, and its own thread's transcript from the top through the assistant blocks that answered this turn. This is quadratic in tokens over a thread, and at prototype scale that is affordable.
54:It is also arranged to be **prefix-stable**, which takes most of the sting out. Successive interactions in the same thread differ only by a suffix: the prompt is `[breadcrumb + transcript prefix][the turn under the microscope + the questions]`, and the graph overview — the one part that changes on every iteration, since the previous reflection just wrote to it — goes **last**, after the transcript, not first. Put the graph at the top and every prompt in the run is a cache miss.
56:To actually get the cache hit, `reflectPrompt` returns content blocks rather than one string: the stable prefix as one text block carrying `cache_control: { type: "ephemeral" }`, the volatile tail as a second. That means `RunThreadOpts.prompt` widens from `string` to `string | Anthropic.ContentBlockParam[]` (`Thread`'s seed likewise, and `Thread.messages` already drops the seed so nothing renders it). The backend forwards `MessageCreateParams` unexamined, so nothing there changes.
61:- `prefix` — this thread's blocks before the turn, with their addresses. Identical across every interaction in the thread up to the split point, which is what makes the cache work.
65:Root-thread turn 0 has an empty `breadcrumb` and empty `prefix`: the task request is the origin of everything and needs no framing.
69:`graph.render()` is re-read inside the loop, per iteration, so each prompt carries the current overview. The yielded text is discarded; the graph is the output, exactly as before. A failed reflection is recorded and the loop **continues** — one bad turn should not abandon the other thirty — and the count of failures surfaces in the status line.
